  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cole gave them five scoreless innings. The lineup gave back nothing.",
    "what_this_means": "Gerrit Cole did the heavy lifting. 1.80 ERA, five strikeouts, one hit allowed through five innings. That should be enough to win a baseball game. It wasn't. The offense managed one hit across nine innings, struck out twelve times, and never put a runner in scoring position. The bullpen held its own after Cole left, but there was nothing to hold onto. This is a team that can't afford to waste dominant pitching on nights when the bats disappear. Cole's line was sharp enough to beat most teams in baseball. Against Tampa Bay, with the division four and a half games back, it wasn't sharp enough to matter. One hit and twelve strikeouts is not a lineup. It's a surrender.",
    "what_to_watch": "Freddy Peralta takes the mound tomorrow night with a 4.42 ERA. A matchup the Yankees' lineup cannot afford to waste like they did last night. Cam Schlittler counters with a 1.23 ERA over his last four starts, giving them a chance on the mound if the bats show up this time.",
